# Pull the panel's media cache from BOTH media shares (OI-15, ruled 2026-07-29;
# two sources since OI-18 exit (b), ruled 2026-08-03).
#
# THIS UNIT SYNCS BOTH FLOWS — music (from HOMEHUB) and frame video (from
# Mini-serv). The bare script with no `--only` is the "everything, now" run, so
# the dedicated on-demand command below means exactly what it always meant.
# The frame flow ALSO has its own every-minute cadence, which is a separate unit
# and timer (wall-sync-frame.{service,timer}) because storage-map §4d gives the
# two flows different cadences and this one's timeout is sized for a full music
# library over Wi-Fi. See that service file for the decision in full.
#
# THREE TRIGGERS FOR THIS UNIT, AND DELIBERATELY NO TIMER ON IT:
#   * ONCE AFTER BOOT — this unit, WantedBy=multi-user.target, ordered after the
#     network is actually up (the panel is Wi-Fi-only, so "network-online" is not
#     a formality here: an rsync started before association just fails).
#   * ON EVERY RESUME — wall-sync-resume.service (OI-16, the Owner chose (a),
#     2026-07-29): the D-W4 window suspends the panel nightly and a resume is not
#     a boot, so the resume hook `systemctl start --no-block`s THIS unit. Same
#     unit, same journal, no second code path.
#   * ON DEMAND — `sudo systemctl start wall-sync.service`. That IS the dedicated
#     SSH-invocable command the ruling calls for: starting a oneshot again re-runs
#     it, so there is nothing else to install and no second entry point that could
#     drift from the boot path.
# The ruling (as amended by OI-16a) is boot + resume + on demand for the MUSIC
# pull it was written for, so no .timer is attached to THIS unit. Resume wrinkle:
# network-online.target is not re-evaluated on wake, so wall-sync.sh does its own
# short bounded nm-online wait before mounting.
[Unit]
Description=Wall panel: mirror both media shares (HOMEHUB music + Mini-serv frame video) into the local cache
Documentation=file:///opt/wall-panel/stack/autoinstall/wall/README.md
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target
# Ordering only (not a dependency): if this is the very first boot, firstboot is
# what renders netplan and the rest of the panel's config, and there is no point
# racing it for the Wi-Fi link.
After=wall-firstboot.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/wall-sync.sh
# THIS UNIT'S TIMEOUT IS A BACKSTOP, NOT THE THING THAT BOUNDS EITHER FLOW.
# It used to be the only bound, and that made the two flows' hangs dependent:
# music runs first, and a music mount or rsync wedged on a half-dead Wi-Fi link
# would burn the whole hour, after which systemd killed the process — so the
# frame flow never ran at all, in a unit whose header promises that one flow's
# trouble does not cancel the other. wall-sync.sh now gives each flow its own
# mount/rsync/unmount budget (flow_spec's F_MOUNT_TIMEOUT / F_RSYNC_TIMEOUT) and
# aggregates the verdicts, so a wedged music mirror fails ITSELF, by name, and
# the frame flow still runs in the same pass.
#
# The arithmetic this number has to cover:
#   music  mount 60 s + rsync 3000 s + unmount 30 s   = 3090 s
#   frame  probe 4 s + mount 20 s + rsync 90 s + unmount 30 s = 144 s
#   total worst case                                  = 3234 s
# 3600 s leaves headroom for the manifest walks and the nm-online wait. The FIRST
# sync copies the whole music library plus the frame set over 802.11 from a 2016
# laptop's radio; later ones are deltas. Generous, because the failure mode of a
# too-short timeout is a half-mirrored cache with a red unit every boot.
TimeoutStartSec=3600
# Bounded IO priority: the panel is drawing a UI and (once synced) playing music
# while this runs, and a full first sync should not make the wall stutter.
IOSchedulingClass=idle
Nice=10
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
